+  // while this destination demands [2,4,6].
+  claim
+    <k>
+      #loadAll(solutionModule)
+      ~> Call(Name("generate_integers"), (Int(A), Int(B), .Exprs))
+      => ref(0)
+    </k>
+    <env> 0 </env>
+    <scopes>
+      ( 0  |-> scope(.Map, parent(-1))
+        -1 |-> builtinsScope
+      )
+      =>
+      ( 0  |-> scope(
+                  "generate_integers" |-> generateIntegersClosure,
+                  parent(-1)
+                )
+        -1 |-> builtinsScope
+      )
+    </scopes>
+    <scopeLoc> 1 </scopeLoc>
+    <heap>
+      .Map
+      =>
+      0 |-> list(vCons(2, evenDigits(A, B)))
+    </heap>
+    <heapLoc> 0 => 1 </heapLoc>
+    <stack> .List </stack>
+    <ret> noRet </ret>
+    <exc> NoExc </exc>
+    <exit-code> 0 </exit-code>
+    requires A >Int 0 andBool B >Int 0
+endmodule

codex
The mutation behaved exactly as required: it parsed and dry-ran successfully, then `kprove` exited `1` with `WarnStuckClaimState` because the false postcondition demanded an extra leading `2`. The concrete witness `(3, 7)` returns `[4, 6]` in both Python implementations while the mutation demands `[2, 4, 6]`. I’m consolidating the trust ledger and final seven-stage review now.
